  "atoms": [
    {
      "id": "a0",
      "pos": 1,
      "text": "Cognitivism holds that moral judgments express beliefs.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a1",
      "pos": 2,
      "text": "Non-cognitivism holds that moral judgments express conative states such as approval and disapproval.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a2",
      "pos": 3,
      "text": "Moral judgments express the speaker's beliefs about how things are.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a3",
      "pos": 4,
      "text": "Moral judgments express the speaker's desires or preferences.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a4",
      "pos": 5,
      "text": "Moral realism asserts that moral facts obtain independently of human attitudes.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a5",
      "pos": 6,
      "text": "On moral realism, whether a moral fact holds does not depend on what anyone approves of.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a6",
      "pos": 7,
      "text": "A.J. Ayer defended emotivism in Language, Truth and Logic.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a7",
      "pos": 8,
      "text": "Ayer maintained that moral statements are literal descriptions of natural facts.",
      "factual": false,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a8",
      "pos": 9,
      "text": "Peter Geach drew on Frege's distinction between the content of a sentence and the force with which it is uttered.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a9",
      "pos": 10,
      "text": "The Frege-Geach problem requires expressivists to explain the meaning of moral sentences embedded in conditionals and arguments.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a10",
      "pos": 11,
      "text": "The embedding objection to expressivism was first formulated by Immanuel Kant.",
      "factual": false,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a11",
      "pos": 12,
      "text": "Simon Blackburn developed quasi-realism to earn the right to moral truth-talk within an expressivist framework.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a12",
      "pos": 13,
      "text": "Mackie's error theory classifies moral judgments as truth-apt.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a13",
      "pos": 14,
      "text": "Mackie's error theory classifies moral judgments as lacking truth-aptness.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    }
  ],
