  "response": "A final consideration frequently taken to settle matters comes from the error-theoretic middle path, which concedes the surface grammar of moral discourse while denying that any moral property is instantiated. Either Mackie's error theory classifies moral judgments as truth-apt, or Mackie's error theory classifies moral judgments as lacking truth-aptness: one of these must be the correct description of the theory, and it cannot be both. How that question is answered determines whether the error theorist counts as an ally of the fact-reporting camp about the semantics of moral language while opposing it about moral metaphysics.\n\nPeter Geach drew on Frege's distinction between the content of a sentence and the force with which it is uttered, and that appeal gave rise to the Frege-Geach problem, which requires expressivists to explain the meaning of moral sentences embedded in conditionals and arguments. The embedding objection to expressivism was first formulated by Immanuel Kant; this origin cannot be squared with Geach's appeal to the Fregean separation of a sentence's content from the force of its utterance, for the objection cannot have been first framed in two such different settings. The requirement that expressivists supply meanings for moral sentences occurring inside conditionals and inside arguments led Simon Blackburn to develop quasi-realism, which is designed to earn the right to moral truth-talk within an expressivist framework.\n\nThe classical statement of the attitude-expressing view belongs to the logical positivist tradition. A.J. Ayer defended emotivism in Language, Truth and Logic. Ayer maintained that moral statements are literal descriptions of natural facts; yet an emotivist treatment of moral language and a construal of moral statements as literal descriptions of natural facts cannot both be correct, since a sentence whose function is to evince feeling is thereby ruled out as a description of how nature stands.\n\nA further question concerns the metaphysical commitments taken on by the fact-reporting side of the debate. Moral realism asserts that moral facts obtain independently of human attitudes; that is to say, on moral realism, whether a moral fact holds does not depend on what anyone approves of. The strength of the realist option is usually measured by whether such attitude-independent facts can be accommodated within a defensible account of the natural world.\n\nThe controversy is standardly framed as one between cognitivism and non-cognitivism about moral discourse. Cognitivism holds that moral judgments express beliefs, whereas non-cognitivism holds that moral judgments express conative states such as approval and disapproval. Adjudication accordingly turns on how moral utterance is to be characterized, and at least one of the following must hold, perhaps both: moral judgments express the speaker's beliefs about how things are; moral judgments express the speaker's desires or preferences, which is simply the non-cognitivist thesis put in other words.",
